{
  "id": "COMPOUND:29444 - PROTEIN:5792",
  "verbalization": "Laxanthone-I is implicated in the regulation of RWLeu4 through a series of molecular interactions involving sialyltransferase activity. The drug acts as a target for multiple enzymes, including CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is a key enzyme in the sialylation pathway. This enzyme is targeted by several compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and (1S,3E,4S,5S,6S)-4,5-dihydroxy-3-(3-methylbut-2-enylidene)-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptan-2-one, which are both known to inhibit sialyltransferase. These inhibitors, in turn, target RWLeu4, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Laxanthone-I may modulate the activity of these enzymes and influence RWLeu4. The consistent evidence across the three paths indicates a direct, mechanistic link between Laxanthone-I and RWLeu4, mediated through the sialyltransferase pathway. The high scores in the final scores suggest that the relationship is robust and well-supported by the available data.",
